Looking at the geometry of the scene, the spectator stands rise directly behind the far touchline (the line of advertising boards and fencing). So if they collapsed forward onto the pitch, the debris and spectators would land first on whichever players are closest to that sideline.

Those players are:

- **The players bound together in the scrum** (the huddle of green‑ and blue‑jerseyed players in the middle of the image), who are packed together just a few metres in front of the advertising boards;
- **The blue‑jerseyed player standing just behind/beside the scrum**, right at the edge of the boards;
- **The green‑jerseyed player standing to the right of the scrum** (near the red advertising panels);
- **The players at the far left and far right edges of the frame** (the blue player on the left touchline and the green and blue players near the boards on the right), who are also hugging the sideline.

By contrast, the green‑jerseyed number 21 standing in open grass in the foreground is the farthest from the stands, so he would be the least likely to be covered.

In short: the spectators would fall on top of the players clustered nearest the far touchline — above all the players locked in the scrum and those standing immediately beside the advertising boards — while players positioned deeper in the field would be out of reach.

